    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let's listen to their conversation.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Ты жарить не умеешь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You cannot fry.' The key word here means 'you', which Senya combines with 'fry'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Мясо жёсткое, как подошва.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The meat is tough as a shoe sole.' The first content word means 'meat', and 'shoe sole' rounds out the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы на флоте сапоги ели.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'In the navy we ate boots.' You'll hear 'we' all the time in the show, here connected with 'were eating'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "И ремни тоже ели.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And we ate belts too.' Notice the word order: 'belt' comes first, and 'were eating' finishes the sentence.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Ты врёшь про сапоги.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You are lying about the boots.' The key word here means 'you', which Senya combines with 'are lying'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не веришь мне?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You don't believe me?' In Russian, 'do you believe' leads into 'to me' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давай поспорим на пять штук.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let's bet five grand.' A compact expression built around 'let's' and 'pieces/grand'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Съешь мой ремень за пять штук?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You'll eat my belt for five grand?' In Russian, 'eat' leads into 'pieces/grand' in a very natural everyday phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну, съем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, I'll eat it.' Fedya starts with 'to eat up' and pairs it with 'to eat up' to make the point clear. Then Senya replies.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Луи, разбей.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Louis, break it.' Senya puts 'Louis' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'to break'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "louis",
      "t": "Хорошо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Okay.' The key word here means 'good', which Louis combines with 'good'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Держи ремень.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Here is the belt.' Here 'here you go' sets up the idea, while 'belt' comes at the end for emphasis. Now let's hear the whole thing once more.",
      "gap": 0.9
